## book/09-evolution-of-a-blog.md

**Goal:** Differentiate this clearly from Chapter 1 by focusing on *Voice/Authority* rather than *Eras/Time*.

```markdown:book/09-evolution-of-a-blog.md
<<<<<<< ORIGINAL
14|## Three Eras, One Arc
15|
16|The evolution has three distinct phases:
17|
18|**Early (2008-2012):** High volume, short posts. A working consultant's notebook—software development tips alongside mathematical observations. Math accounted for only 21% of posts. The blog was figuring out what it wanted to be.
19|
20|**Middle (2013-2017):** Volume dropped sharply (147 posts in 2014, the quietest year), but depth increased. Math rose to 35%, then 65%. PowerShell disappeared. Clinical trials faded. The professional context was being shed.
21|
22|**Recent (2018-2025):** Volume recovered while depth remained. Math now dominates at 60-70%. Posts average 450 words—double the early era. The voice is confident, the focus clear.
=======
14|## From Notebook to Institution
15|
16|The change isn't just about volume; it's about authority.
17|
18|**The Notebook Phase (2008-2012):** The early blog reads like a consultant's scratchpad. "Here's a fix for a PowerShell bug." "Here's a link to an article." The voice is helpful but tentative. It's a professional sharing notes.
19|
20|**The Clarification (2013-2017):** As the professional focus shifted, the voice tightened. The "tips and tricks" vanished. The mathematical explanations grew longer. The author stopped pointing at other people's work and started synthesizing his own.
21|
22|**The Authority (2018-2025):** The modern blog doesn't just explain topics; it connects them. A post about the gamma function references a post about statistical mechanics which references a post about Python code. The voice is no longer just helpful; it is authoritative. The blog has become a reference work.
>>>>>>> REVISED
```

